## 25. How everything connects

Understanding the relationships between pages is more important than memorizing their names.

Consider this example:

A tourist wants to rent a Toyota Land Cruiser for three days.

## Complete booking lifecycle

1. Customer visits the storefront

   Selects dates, vehicle category and pickup location.
2. Availability engine checks the fleet

   Checks the actual vehicle inventory and overlapping reservations.
3. Booking is created

   A booking reference is generated and the request is recorded.
4. Booking agent confirms the booking

   Confirms the customer request and applicable price.
5. Dispatcher assigns resources

   Selects an available vehicle and driver.
6. Driver receives instructions

   Receives trip details through the configured notification channel.
7. Trip begins

   Driver checks in and updates the trip status.
8. Trip is completed

   Vehicle becomes available again after the rental period and any required checks.
9. Financial records are updated

   Payment records, booking value and outstanding balances are updated.
10. Owner reviews performance

    The completed transaction contributes to reports and analytics.

This is the central workflow that the entire product must support.

